# Chapter 3, last part: the Print Error method of Search and the macros and
# the library of the Tools menu.  The markers that the method finds are
# described in chapter 5 (markers-errors) and made by Print and Preview
# (chapter 6, print-errors).

.h2 Find Next Error [edit-c-find-error]
.ix Find Next Error
.ix Find Previous Error
.ix Next Error
.ix Previous Error
.ix Search method
.ix Search, Find
.ix Search again
.ix Ctrl-F9
.ix Ctrl-F8
.ix Ctrl-F7
.ix Remove, Print Error
.ix Remove All, Print Errors
.ix Errors menu
.ix Print Error, find
Find Next Error is the second method of Search.  Where the method Text looks
for a string of characters ({n:edit-b-search}), the method Print Error looks
for the next Print Error marker ({n:markers-errors}): the notes that Print and
Preview leave in the text at each place where the document could not be
typeset as it is written ({n:print-errors}).  Use it after a Print or a
Preview: go from one marker to the next, read the message, correct the
document there, and go on; when the last one is mended, print again.

.h3 How to reach it
.table 23 37
Way | What it does
{k:Ctrl-F8} | Find the next Print Error.  Chooses the method Print Error and the
 direction Forward.
{k:Ctrl-F7} | Find the previous Print Error.  Chooses the method Print Error and
 the direction Backward.
{k:Ctrl-F9} | Search again: repeats the last search with its method and
 direction.  After {k:Ctrl-F8} it finds the next error, after {k:Ctrl-F7} the
 previous one; after a search for text, the next or previous match.
{k:F9} | The Search panel, with the fields Search for, Find, Direction, Match
 Case, Whole Word and Attribute.
{k:F12}, Print, Errors, Next | The same as {k:Ctrl-F8}.
{k:F12}, Print, Errors, Previous | The same as {k:Ctrl-F7}.
{k:F12}, Print, Errors, Remove | Take out the Print Error marker under the cursor.
{k:F12}, Print, Errors, Remove All | Take out every Print Error marker.
.end
The four entries make the submenu Errors of the Print menu
({n:print-menu}), after Draft, Final and Preview.
They are reached with {k:F12}, {k:P}, {k:E} and the cursor keys; the Print row
and the Errors row each fit in one row of the menu bar.  The hint line shows which entry is chosen and shows "Find
the next Print Error [Ctrl-F8]", "Find the previous Print Error [Ctrl-F7]",
"Remove the Print Error under the cursor" and "Remove all the Print Errors".
Search itself is reached with the keys and the panel; Find Next Error also
with the Print menu.

{k:Ctrl-F7}, {k:Ctrl-F8} and {k:Ctrl-F9} are commands of their own.  When the
terminal version does not receive them, the Print menu and the Search panel do
the same.

.h3 The Search panel
The panel is titled {b:Search}.  It is a form like the panels of the menus:
{k:Down}, {k:Tab} and {k:Enter} go to the next field, {k:Up} to the previous
one, {k:Space}, {k:Left} and {k:Right} change a choice, {k:Ins} accepts and
{k:Esc} cancels ({n:view-form-keys}).  In the terminal version it
looks like this.
.code
 +-------------------------- Search --------------------------+
 |  Search for: ......................................        |
 |                                                            |
 |        Find: [Print Error]                                 |
 |                                                            |
 |   Direction: [Forward]                                     |
 |                                                            |
 |   Attribute: [Any]                                         |
 |                                                            |
 |     Ins accepts, Esc cancels                               |
 +------------------------------------------------------------+
.end
Match Case and Whole Word do not show while Find is Print Error.
.pic pictures/edit-c-search-panel.png 5 The Search panel (F9) with Find at Print Error.
.table 10 16 33
Field | Values | Meaning
Search for | Up to 79 characters | The string for the method Text.  The field
 begins with the string used last, and the first printing key typed
 replaces it.  The method Print Error does not need it; a string that is
 typed is kept all the same for the next Text search and for Replace.
Find | Text (default), Print Error, Tab, End of Line, Nonbreaking Space,
 Marker, Soft Hyphen | The method.  Text finds the string, Print Error finds
 the next Print Error marker; the others find a tab, an end of line, a
 nonbreaking space, a marker or a soft hyphen ({n:edit-b-search}).
Direction | Forward (default), Backward | Forward looks after the cursor and
 goes on from the start of the document; Backward looks before the cursor
 and goes on from the end.
Match Case | No (default), Yes | Yes needs the same case of letters.  Only
 for Find at Text.
Whole Word | No (default), Yes | Yes needs a text that does not touch a letter
 or a digit.  Only for Find at Text.
Attribute | Any (default), Plain, Bold, Italics, Underscore | Finds only text
 with that attribute.
.end
The panel opens with the method and the direction that were chosen last, by
the panel or by {k:Ctrl-F7} and {k:Ctrl-F8}: after {k:Ctrl-F7} it shows Print
Error and Backward.  Accepting it makes the choice stay, and searches at
once.  With Find at Text and an empty Search for, nothing is searched; the
choices are kept.  {k:Esc} changes nothing.

.h3 What it does
The cursor goes to the first marker after the cursor, in the order of the
document (before the cursor for the previous one) and is put on the marker.
A marker under the cursor is not found again.  The status line shows the new
place, and the message line shows
.code
Print error 3 of 4: MESSAGE [code]
.end
the place of the marker among all of them in the document, counted from the
start, and its message and code.  The message line is cut at the width of
the window; {k:Alt-F12} on the marker shows all of the text
({n:markers-errors}).  On the pages of the print the same fault has a label
with its number in the Error Log ({n:print-failed}); the two numbers are the
same when the whole document was printed and has not been changed since.  A
fault that has no place in the document (an error that cannot be
traced to a block) has an entry in the Error Log and the banner, but no
marker, so the markers may be fewer than the entries.
.table 23 37
Case | What happens
After the last marker, going forward | The search goes round to the first and
 the message begins "Wrapped to the start.", then "Print error 1 of 4: ...".
Before the first, going backward | The same the other way: "Wrapped to the
 end.  Print error 4 of 4: ...".
Only one marker, the cursor on it | It is found again, with the wrap message.
No marker in the document | The cursor stays and the message line shows "No
 print errors".
.end

.h3 Search again
{k:Ctrl-F9} repeats the last search with the method and the direction that
were chosen.  For Print Error it finds the next marker (the previous one when
the direction is Backward).  For Text it searches in the direction chosen for
the string of the last Search or Replace, and the message is "Found" or
"Not found: " and the string.  With no string yet, the message line shows
"Nothing to search for: use Search (F9) first".

.h3 Taking markers out
Remove takes out the marker under the cursor and shows "Print Error
removed"; with the cursor on anything else the message line shows "The cursor
is not on a Print Error".  Remove All takes out all of them and shows
"4 print errors removed" ("1 print error removed"), or "No print errors" when
there
were none.  {k:Del} with the cursor on a marker and {k:Backspace} just after
it take out that marker as well.

.h3 Messages of Find Next Error
.table 28 30
Message | Meaning
Print error N of M: MESSAGE [code] | The cursor is on the Nth of M markers.
Wrapped to the start.  Print error 1 of M: ... | The search went round the end.
Wrapped to the end.  Print error M of M: ... | The search went round the start.
No print errors | None in the document (also from Remove All).
Print Error removed | Remove did its work.
The cursor is not on a Print Error | Remove with the cursor elsewhere.
N print errors removed | Remove All took out N markers.
Nothing to search for: use Search ({k:F9}) first | {k:Ctrl-F9} before any text search.
Found | The text was found.
Found: the search wrapped to the start of the document | The text was found after the search went round the end of the document.
Found: the search wrapped to the end of the document | The same, backward, round the start.
Not found: STRING | The text is not in the document.
.end

.h2 Case [edit-c-case]
.ix Case
.ix Upper case
.ix Lower case
.ix Capitalize
.ix Ctrl-F6
{k:Ctrl-F6} changes the case of letters.  A box offers {b:Upper}, {b:Lower} or
{b:Capitalize}; then, as for Copy, a second box prompts for what to change
(Text,
Column, Block or Section), the unit is highlighted with the cursor keys, and
{k:Enter} makes the change; {k:Esc} cancels.  If you press {k:Enter} at once on
Text, with nothing highlighted, the word under the cursor is changed.
.pic pictures/edit-c-case-menu.png 5 The box of Case: Upper, Lower and Capitalize.
.pic pictures/edit-c-case-unit.png 5 The second box of Case, which prompts for what to change.
.table 10 50
Choice | What it does
Upper | Every letter becomes a capital.
Lower | Every letter becomes a small letter.
Capitalize | The first letter of each word becomes a capital and the other
 | letters small letters.  A word is letters, digits and apostrophes, so
 | {c:don't} becomes {c:Don't} and {c:well-known} becomes {c:Well-Known}.
.end
Only letters change.  Digits, spaces, tabs, ends of lines, attributes and
markers (the Print Error ones too) stay as they are.  A word that begins with
a digit keeps its small letters in Capitalize: {c:3rd} stays {c:3rd}.  The letters are those of
the PC character set: the pairs of A to Z, and of the letters with an accent
that it has in both cases (the e with an acute accent, the umlauts of a, o and
u, the ring a, the cedilla c, the ligature ae and the tilde n).  The other
letters with an accent, and the German sharp s, have no other case in the
character set and stay.  The change is one operation for {b:Undo} ({k:Ctrl-/}),
named Case.  The messages are in {x:msg-editor-line}.
.h2 Border [edit-c-border]
.ix Border
.ix Border Layout
.ix Alt-F8
.ix Borders of columns
.ix Gutter
.ix Shading
{k:Alt-F8} puts lines around columns and blocks.  The message line prompts you
to highlight the column or columns with the cursor keys (the highlight runs
from
where the cursor was when you pressed the key to where it is now, over the
columns and the blocks between the two) and to press {k:Enter}; {k:Esc}
cancels.  The panel {b:Border Layout} then opens.
.table 23 37
Field | What it sets
All | Every border: the four sides of each column, and the lines between the
 | columns.  None, Single, Double or Wide.
Outside | The four outer sides of the highlight.
Top, Bottom | The top and the bottom of every column.
Left, Right | The left side of the first column and the right side of the last.
Inside | The lines between the columns; n/a for one column.
Top Gutter, Bottom Gutter | The space above and below the blocks, in inches
 | with up to three decimals (0.250).
Connect Borders? | Yes makes the borders of the blocks close up.
Shading (%) | The share of grey in the area, 0 to 100.
.end
.pic pictures/edit-b-border-panel.png 5 The Border Layout panel, with the top and the bottom double.
{k:Ins} uses the settings that you changed; {k:Shift-Ins} uses every setting.
All is applied first, then Outside and Inside, and the sides last, so that a
side that you set wins over All.  A gutter with more than three decimals is
refused: {c:Invalid number (no more than 3 decimal places allowed)}.  A shading
below 0 or above 100 is refused: {c:Shading must be from 0 to 100}.  The change
is one operation for {b:Undo}, named Border.  Borders are written in the file.
The screen shows them at once, in the gap at each side of
a column and on the row above and below the block: a thin line for Single, a
double line for Double and a bold double line for Wide.  Where lines meet it
draws the corner, the tee or the crossing that the character set has for them: a
corner at the four corners of a table, a tee where the side of a column meets
the line between two rows, and a crossing where two lines cross (the line
between two rows goes on across a column that has no border, so the side of a
bordered column next to it makes a crossing there and not a tee), in single,
double and mixed forms (where a single line meets a double one the set has no
character for every case, and the heavier line is drawn).  A terminal that
cannot show them gets a plus sign, a hyphen, an equals sign for a double line
and a vertical bar.  The text does not move,
they follow the columns and the lines of a block that wraps, a folded section
hides them, and {b:Undo} and {b:Redo} take them away and bring them back.  The
Printer draws them on the page too ({n:print-borders}).
.table 28 26
Message | When
Border: N changes | The panel was accepted ("Border: 1 change" for one).
Cancelled | The panel was cancelled with {k:Esc}.
There is no block to put a border on | The highlight holds no block.
There is no column to put a border on | The highlight holds no column.
.end
.h3 Borders on several columns and rows [edit-c-border-many]
.ix Borders on several columns and rows
.ix Border, highlight of several rows
The highlight of Border is a rectangle.  Press {k:Alt-F8}, then move the
cursor with {k:Shift-Right} and {k:Shift-Left} to take in more columns and
with {k:Down} or {k:Up} to take in more rows (blocks).  Every column of the
rectangle is shown highlighted, and the settings of the panel go to all of
them.  The panel opens with what the highlighted columns have: a field where
they all agree shows that value, and a field where they differ shows n/a (or,
for a gutter or the shading, is empty).  A field that you leave at n/a or empty
changes nothing, even with {k:Shift-Ins}, so the columns that differed keep
what they had.  The document is marked as changed once and a single
{b:Undo} takes back the borders of the whole rectangle.  With nothing
highlighted beyond the cursor, the panel works on the one column as before.

.pic pictures/edit-c-border-table.png 5 The table of the example, before any border.

As an example, take a table of three columns and five rows in which the top
row has a single border on every side except the bottom, where it has a
double line, and the other four rows have a single border on every side
except the top.
1. Put the cursor in the first column of the first row and press
   {k:Alt-F8}.  Press {k:Shift-Right} twice, to take in the three columns,
   and {k:Enter}.
2. Set All to Single and Bottom to Double, and press {k:Ins}.
3. Put the cursor in the first column of the second row and press
   {k:Alt-F8}.  Press {k:Shift-Right} twice and {k:Down} three times, to take
   in the four remaining rows, and {k:Enter}.
4. Set Left, Inside, Right and Bottom to Single, leave Top as it is (None),
   and press {k:Ins}.
.pic pictures/edit-c-border-highlight.png 5 The first two rows highlighted over their three columns: the rectangle that Border works on.

.pic pictures/edit-c-border-example.png 5 The table of the example after the two steps.

Top is left alone in step 4 because the rows have no top border; to take away
a top border that was there before, set Top to None.
The line between the first row and the second is the bottom of the first row,
and the top of the second row has none, so one double line is drawn there.
Where two neighbors disagree about a line that they share (the bottom of one
row and the top of the next), the Editor and the Printer both draw the
stronger one, and a double or
wide line wins over a single one.

.pic pictures/print-border-page.png 5 A table whose cells have a double border, as it is printed.
.h2 Math [edit-c-math]
.ix Math
.ix Calculator
.ix Ctrl-F2
{k:Ctrl-F2} calculates the numbers in the document.  As for Copy, a box prompts
for what to calculate (Text, Column, Block or Section), the unit is highlighted
with the cursor keys, and {k:Enter} calculates; {k:Esc} cancels.  The result is
put on the clipboard, so that {b:Paste} ({k:F5}) puts it in the text; paste it
before you cut or copy anything else.  The message line shows {c:Result: 14}.
The
document is not changed, so there is nothing to undo.  Text with nothing
highlighted has no numbers: the message line prompts you to highlight them.
.pic pictures/edit-c-math-unit.png 5 The box of Math, which prompts for what to calculate.
.pic pictures/edit-c-math-result.png 5 The result of four prices on the message line.
.table 17 43
In the highlight | What Math does
Numbers | Whole numbers and decimals of up to 100 digits.  The decimal
 | separator is the period; a comma is one too when it stands between digits
 | of a number that has no period ({c:1,5}).  A comma in a number that has a
 | period is left out ({c:1,234.50}).
Operators | In the order of their precedence: parentheses (), the exponent ^
 | (a whole number), negation -, multiplication and division * and /,
 | addition and subtraction + and -.
% | Divides the number before it by 100.
e or E | A power of ten, as in {c:1.5e3} (1500) or {c:2E-2}; the sign may be
 | there.
No operator | Numbers with no operator between them are added: {c:2 3}
 | gives 5, and {c:2 3 * 4} gives 14.
Other characters | Ignored: letters, signs of money and the like.
.end
The numbers of several columns or blocks are taken from left to right and from
top to bottom.  The arithmetic is exact: {c:0.1 + 0.2} is 0.3.  The result has
as
many decimals as it needs, without a zero at the end, and at most 100 digits
({c:1 / 3} gives 0.333... with 100 threes); a result with more than 100 digits
before the point is written with a power of ten ({c:1E+100}).  A division by
zero
is refused with {c:Cannot divide by zero} and the clipboard is not changed.  A
number of more than 100 digits, or an expression that is not good, such as
parentheses that do not match, is refused with {c:Cannot calculate: the
expression is not good}; a text with no number gives {c:There is no number to
calculate}.  The message line shows a long result from its right end, with
three periods in front; the clipboard holds the whole result.  The messages
are in {x:msg-editor-line}.
.h2 Tools: Macros and Library [edit-c-tools]
.ix Tools, Macros
.ix Tools, Library
.ix Macros
.ix Library
.ix Macro key
.ix Alt-letter macro
.ix Keystrokes of a macro
.ix Macro set
.ix Library entry
.ix Lib Copy
.ix Lib Cut
.ix Lib Paste
.ix Copy to Library
.ix Paste from Library
The first two entries of the Tools menu keep two stores that live as long as
the Editor runs, whichever document is being edited: the macros, and the
library.  A macro is a recorded series of keys that runs again when {k:Alt}
and its key are pressed.  The library holds named pieces of text, blocks and
sections that can be pasted into any document.  Both can be saved to a file
and retrieved from one.  The other entries
of the menu are described with Autosort ({n:edit-b-sort}), Count
({n:edit-b-words}) and, for Spell and Thesaurus, {n:spell}.

Every panel of this section is closed with {k:Ins}, which accepts it, or
{k:Esc}, which cancels it.  In a field that names an entry, a file or a
directory, {k:Keypad +} or {k:F2} shows a list to choose from.

.h3 Tools, Macros, Learn [edit-c-macro-learn]
.ix Learn, macro
.ix Define Macro
.ix Ctrl-F1, end of a macro
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Learn starts the recording of a macro; so does
{k:Ctrl-F1}.  The panel Define Macro prompts for three fields; accepting it
starts the recording, and the message line shows
"Defining Alt-K: press Ctrl-F1 to end" (K is the macro key).
.table 20 40
Field | What it holds
Macro Key: Alt- | One letter or one digit.  A letter is kept as a capital, so
 that {k:Alt-q} and {k:Alt-Q} are the same macro.  Anything else is refused with "The
 macro key is a letter or a digit".  A key that already has a macro is
 recorded anew.
Description | A line of text that says what the macro is for.  May be empty.
Include in Macro Set? | No or Yes.  The initial value is No.  A macro that
 is not included still runs while the Editor runs, but Save leaves it out of
 the file.
.end
Until {k:Ctrl-F1} is pressed, every key is kept, in menus and panels too, and
it also does what it always does.  The one exception is {k:F12}, the key of the
menu bar, which is never kept.  {k:Ctrl-F1} ends the recording and is not
part of the macro.  {k:Shift} is not kept: {k:Shift-Down} is recorded as {k:Down}.  To
run the macro, press {k:Alt} and its key ({k:Alt-Q} for the example).  The macro
keys are {k:Alt-A} to {k:Alt-Z} and {k:Alt-0} to {k:Alt-9}; A macro on a key that
is also a command ({k:Alt-I}, {k:Alt-O}, {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9}) is run instead
of the command for as long as the macro exists.
A macro keeps the keys, not the names of the entries that were chosen.  Because
{k:F12} is never kept, a macro cannot open the menu bar.  The keys {k:F10} and
{k:F11} are kept like the others.

.h3 Tools, Macros, Edit [edit-c-macro-edit]
.ix Macro Edit
.ix Keystrokes, edit
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Edit looks at a macro and changes it.  A small panel,
Macro Edit, prompts for the Macro Key first; the second panel, Macro Edit again,
has four fields:
.table 20 40
Field | What it holds
Macro Key: Alt- | The key, as above.  Changing it makes a copy under the new
 key.
Description | The line of text.
Keystrokes | The recorded keys as one line of text.  A character is itself.
 The less-than sign is written twice.  Any other key is its name in angle
 brackets: {c:<Enter> <Tab> <Esc> <BkSp> <Ins> <Del>}
 {c:<Up> <Down> <Left> <Right> <Home> <End> <PgUp>}
 {c:<PgDn>}, {c:<F1>} to {c:<F11>} and {c:<NBSP>} (the nonbreaking space);
 with Alt- or Ctrl- in front of the name a key that was pressed with Alt or Ctrl, such as {c:<Alt-Q>},
 {c:<Ctrl-B>} or {c:<Ctrl-Right>}.  A line that cannot be read is refused
 with a notice that begins "The keystrokes are not understood" and explains
 the angle brackets.
Include in Macro Set? | No or Yes: the macro is written by Save or not.  A
 macro that is not there yet starts with No.
.end
The message line then shows "Macro Alt-K has N keystrokes".

.h3 Tools, Macros, Delete [edit-c-macro-delete]
.ix Delete Macro
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Delete prompts in the panel Delete Macro for one field and
then "Delete the macro Alt-K?".
.table 16 44
Field | What it holds
Macro Key: Alt- | The key of the macro to delete: one letter or digit.
.end
With no macro a message box shows "There are no macros", and a key that has
none is answered by the box "No such macro".  The message line shows "Deleted".

.h3 Tools, Macros, Save [edit-c-macro-save]
.ix Save Macro Set
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Save writes the macros that are included into a macro file
The panel Save Macro Set has five fields.
.table 17 43
Field | What it holds
Working Directory | The directory of the file ({n:file-workdir}); it becomes the
 working directory of the Editor.
File Name | The name of the file.  A name with no period gets the extension
 {c:.mac}.  It must be given: "File must be specified (File Name)".
Description | A line of text kept in the file.
Author | The author, kept in the file.
Revision | A number with up to three decimals, such as 1.000.  When it is not
 a number above 0 the revision is the old one plus 0.001, or 1.000 the first
 time.
.end
When the file exists, a prompt reads "Overwrite old version on disk?".  The
message line shows "Saved" and the path.

.h3 Tools, Macros, Retrieve [edit-c-macro-retrieve]
.ix Retrieve Macro Set
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Retrieve reads a macro file.  The macro set of the
Setup field Initial Macro Set is read in the same way when the Editor starts
({n:str-setup-fields}).  The panel Retrieve Macro Set
has three fields.
.table 17 43
Field | What it holds
Working Directory | The directory of the file.
File Name | The name; the extension {c:.mac} is added to a name with no period.
Options | One of two choices, below.
.end
.table 27 34
Options | Effect
Overwrite Current Macros | All macros that are in memory are deleted first.
Merge with Current Macros | The macros of the file are added; a macro of the
 file replaces one of the same key.
.end
All macros that are read have Include in Macro Set?  Yes.  The message line shows
"Retrieved N macros".  A file that is not a macro set gives "That file is not
a macro set", a file that cannot be read "Cannot read the file".

.h3 Tools, Library, Edit [edit-c-library-edit]
.ix Library Entry
.ix Library, edit an entry
{k:F12}, Tools, Library, Edit looks at a library entry, changes it, or makes a new
one.  First a small panel, Library Entry, prompts for the Entry Name (the list of
{k:Keypad +} or {k:F2} shows the names that exist).  Then the panel Library
Entry has four fields:
.table 19 41
Field | What it holds
Entry Name | The name.  A new name makes a copy of the entry; the entry of the
 old name stays.  An empty name is refused: "Enter the name of the entry".
Description | A line of text.
Contents | Shown as ...: the contents are not typed here.  After the panel a prompt reads "Edit the contents of the entry?" and, when the answer is yes, the contents open in the editor (a section is not edited: it can only be
 pasted or deleted).
Include in Library? | No or Yes.  An entry that exists starts with Yes, a new
 one with No.  Accepting a name with No removes the entry of that name.
.end
The entries are kept in alphabetical order.  The message line shows how many
entries the library has.

.h3 Tools, Library, Delete [edit-c-library-delete]
.ix Delete from Library
{k:F12}, Tools, Library, Delete prompts in the panel Delete from Library for one
field and then "Delete the library entry NAME?".
.table 10 50
Field | What it holds
Entry Name | The name of the entry to delete ({k:Keypad +} or {k:F2} lists
 them).
.end
With an empty library a message box titled Delete from Library shows "The
library has no entries", and a name that does not exist is answered by the box
"No such library entry".

.h3 Tools, Library, Save [edit-c-library-save]
.ix Save Library
{k:F12}, Tools, Library, Save writes the library to a library file, which is a
document.  The panel Save Library has five fields.
.table 17 43
Field | What it holds
Working Directory | The directory of the file ({n:file-workdir}).
File Name | The name of the file.  A name with no period gets the extension
 {c:.lib}.  A missing name gives "No file name specified".
Description | A line of text kept in the file.
Author | The author, kept in the file.
Revision | A number with up to three decimals, such as 1.000.  When it is not
 a number above 0 the revision is the old one plus 0.001, or 1.000 the first
 time.
.end
When the file exists, a prompt reads "Overwrite old version on disk?" before the write.

.h3 Tools, Library, Retrieve [edit-c-library-retrieve]
.ix Retrieve Library
{k:F12}, Tools, Library, Retrieve reads a library file.  The library of the
Setup field Initial Library is read in the same way when the Editor starts
({n:str-setup-fields}).  The panel Retrieve Library
has three fields.
.table 17 43
Field | What it holds
Working Directory | The directory of the file.
File Name | The name; the extension {c:.lib} is added to a name with no period.
Options | One of two choices, below.
.end
.table 28 30
Options | Effect
Overwrite Current Library Entries | The entries in memory are deleted first.
Merge with Current Library Entries | The entries are added to the others.
.end
The message line shows "Retrieved N library entries".  A file that is not a
library gives "That file is not a library".

.h3 Copy to Library and Paste from Library [edit-c-library-keys]
.ix Lib Copy
.ix Lib Cut
.ix Lib Paste
.ix Ctrl-F3
.ix Ctrl-F4
.ix Ctrl-F5
{k:Ctrl-F3} (Lib Copy) and {k:Ctrl-F4} (Lib Cut) take the highlighted text,
column, block or section as {k:F3} and {k:F4} do ({n:edit-copy}, {n:edit-cut})
and then ask for a library entry in the panel Copy to Library:
.table 19 41
Field | What it holds
Entry name | The name of the entry.  Empty: "Enter a name for the entry".  When
 the name exists, a prompt reads "Replace the entry that has this name?".
Description | A line of text.
Include in Library? | Yes at first.  With No nothing is kept and the message
 line shows "Not included in the library".
.end
{k:Ctrl-F5} (Lib Paste) prompts in the panel Paste from Library for the Entry name
and pastes the entry at the cursor as {k:F5} does ({n:edit-b-paste}); the
clipboard of {k:F3} and {k:F4} is not changed.  With an empty library a message box titled
Paste from Library says "The library has no entries"; an unknown name is
answered by the box "No such library entry".  Any key closes a message box.
